Mobility particle size spectrometers — Wiedensohler and colleagues (@cm-mobility-source: 0, is #reference, #part-of @cm-sources) { r_1W06WPADV43B98EF8NSW9RDCWM
A. Wiedensohler and colleagues. *Mobility particle size spectrometers: harmonization of technical standards and data structure to facilitate high quality long-term observations of atmospheric particle number size distributions*, Atmospheric Measurement Techniques 5, 657–685 (2012). [Article, authors and licence](https://amt.copernicus.org/articles/5/657/2012/) · [Twenty-nine-page PDF](https://amt.copernicus.org/articles/5/657/2012/amt-5-657-2012.pdf), DOI 10.5194/amt-5-657-2012.

Read the complete paper, including the instrument principles, flow and humidity observations, loss corrections, comparisons and appendices. Examine the difference between comparing inversion programs with a common ideal transfer function and comparing complete instruments observing a real aerosol.

Instrument-principle, flow/state, loss-correction, inversion-comparison and counting-efficiency passages were inspected. The full experimental comparison, figures and original data were not independently reproduced. The publisher identifies CC BY 3.0; preserve attribution and component credits for any reuse. The atmospheric application, historical editions and instrument-specific examples need their stated conditions when used in manufacturing. The PDF remains external.

